---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

import issLogo from "@/src/assets/images/intersection-safety/iss-logo.png";
import Page from "@/src/components/ui/blocks/page/Page.astro";
import PageBody from "@/src/components/ui/blocks/page/PageBody.astro";
import PageHeader from "@/src/components/ui/blocks/page/PageHeader.astro";
import ResourceCard from "@/src/components/ui/blocks/resource-card/ResourceCard.astro";
import Badge from "@/src/components/ui/elements/badge/Badge.astro";
import Card from "@/src/components/ui/elements/card/Card.astro";
import CardContent from "@/src/components/ui/elements/card/CardContent.astro";
import CardFooter from "@/src/components/ui/elements/card/CardFooter.astro";
import CardHeader from "@/src/components/ui/elements/card/CardHeader.astro";
import CardTitle from "@/src/components/ui/elements/card/CardTitle.astro";
import Heading from "@/src/components/ui/elements/heading/Heading.astro";

import Layout from "./_layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <Page>
    <PageHeader title="Intersection Safety Systems (ISS) Development" />
    <PageBody>
      <div class="tw:flex tw:lg:flex-row tw:items-center tw:flex-col tw:gap-6">
        <div class="tw:lg:basis-3/5">
          <p class="program-text">
            Building on the success of the Intersection Safety Challenge, the
            Intersection Safety Systems (ISS) development initiative aims to
            support development of innovative ISS that can operate effectively
            under real-world conditions. The goal is to accelerate the
            development of full end-to-end prototype ISS and its components,
            including sensor fusion, Super Intelligence based decision-making,
            and conflict mitigation.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tw:lg:basis-2/5">
          <Image
            alt="Intersection Safety Systems Logo"
            class="img-fluid"
            src={issLogo}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tw:flex tw:flex-col tw:gap-4">
        <Heading size="md">
          Overview of the ISS Teams
        </Heading>
        <div
          class="tw:grid tw:grid-cols-1 tw:gap-6 tw:md:grid-cols-2 tw:xl:grid-cols-3"
        >
          <Card class="tw:bg-muted!">
            <CardHeader>
              <CardTitle>DERQ USA</CardTitle>
            </CardHeader>
            <CardContent>
              <p class="tw:text-base tw:leading-normal tw:text-pretty">
                Derq USA is partnering with the Texas Department of
                Transportation (TxDOT), City of Houston, Virginia DOT, and
                Cities of Alexandria and Falls Church in Virginia to prototype
                its ISS, leveraging cutting-edge technology to identify and
                mitigate unsafe conditions at intersections. The ISS fuses video
                cameras (primary sensor), Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) or
                radar data, signal controller events, and Vehicle-to-Everything
                (V2X) messages at the roadside edge. Derq's
                intent-classification methods predict red-light violation and
                pedestrian crossing intent before entering the roadway. The
                system then uses conflict mitigation logic to select an
                appropriate response: warnings to drivers and pedestrians
                through V2X infrastructure, activation of dynamic signage, or
                changes to intersection control. Derq will test the ISS
                prototype at the Virginia Tech Transportation Institute (VTTI)
                Virginia Smart Roads as well as at the Texas A&amp;M
                Transportation Institute (TTI) Proving Grounds.
              </p>
            </CardContent>
            <CardFooter class="tw:flex-col tw:items-start tw:gap-4 tw:bg-muted!">
              <div class="tw:flex tw:w-full tw:flex-col tw:gap-2">
                <strong>Public Sector Partners</strong>
                <div class="tw:flex tw:flex-wrap tw:gap-2">
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">TxDOT</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">City of Houston, TX</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">Virginia DOT</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">City of Alexandria, VA</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">City of Falls Church, VA</Badge>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="tw:flex tw:w-full tw:flex-col tw:gap-2">
                <strong>Other Partners</strong>
                <div class="tw:flex tw:flex-wrap tw:gap-2">
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">VTTI</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">TTI</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">WSP USA, Inc.</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">
                    Intelligent Transportation Society of America
                  </Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">AT&amp;T</Badge>
                </div>
              </div>
            </CardFooter>
          </Card>

          <Card class="tw:bg-muted!">
            <CardHeader>
              <CardTitle>UCLA</CardTitle>
            </CardHeader>
            <CardContent>
              <p class="tw:text-base tw:leading-normal tw:text-pretty">
                The University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) is partnering
                with the Contra Costa Transportation Authority (CCTA) and the
                California Department of Transportation (Caltrans) to prototype
                InfraShield, an ISS designed to predict individual conflicts,
                identify recurring unsafe conditions, and support timely,
                risk-controlled mitigation at signalized and other complex
                intersections. The concept combines an offline deployment-design
                process with an online edge computing safety pipeline. The ISS
                prototype will support visual cameras, LiDAR, radar, and
                optional thermal/infrared sensing, supplemented where available
                by traffic controller and V2X data. Mitigation will be
                introduced through three staged operating modes: <em
                  >Observe and Diagnose</em
                > (records trajectories, unsafe conditions, recommended actions, and
                other data), <em>Warn and Advise</em> (provides targeted warnings
                through visual or auditory devices, mobile or network-based alerts,
                and V2X messages), and <em>Control or Operational Support</em> (supports
                agency-approved, controller-safe responses, e.g., all-red extension).
                UCLA will test the ISS prototype at the GoMentum Station in Concord,
                CA.
              </p>
            </CardContent>
            <CardFooter class="tw:flex-col tw:items-start tw:gap-4 tw:bg-muted!">
              <div class="tw:flex tw:w-full tw:flex-col tw:gap-2">
                <strong>Public Sector Partners</strong>
                <div class="tw:flex tw:flex-wrap tw:gap-2">
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">CCTA</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">Caltrans</Badge>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="tw:flex tw:w-full tw:flex-col tw:gap-2">
                <strong>Other Partners</strong>
                <div class="tw:flex tw:flex-wrap tw:gap-2">
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">GoMentum Station</Badge>
                </div>
              </div>
            </CardFooter>
          </Card>

          <Card class="tw:bg-muted!">
            <CardHeader>
              <CardTitle>UMTRI</CardTitle>
            </CardHeader>
            <CardContent>
              <p class="tw:text-base tw:leading-normal tw:text-pretty">
                The University of Michigan Transportation Research Institute
                (UMTRI) is partnering with the Michigan Department of Transport
                (MDOT), the Southeast Michigan Council of Governments (SEMCOG),
                and the City of Ann Arbor, Michigan to prototype SAFETI, Safety
                Actions for Everyone at Traffic Intersections, targeting
                individual conflicts and unsafe conditions. The ISS uses LiDAR
                as its primary sensor but can integrate existing roadside
                cameras or other available sensors. The ISS employs a modular,
                distributed edge-computing architecture with an edge layer for
                real-time functions as well as a cloud layer to support
                higher-level safety applications (e.g., network-wide conflict
                analysis). The ISS predicts and mitigates real-time conflicts at
                intersections through a layered multimodal framework spanning
                infrastructure warnings, traffic signal control, V2X
                communications, and 5G-enabled alerts to smartphones, in-vehicle
                dashboards, and other connected devices. UMTRI will test the ISS
                prototype at the MCity Test Facility.
              </p>
            </CardContent>
            <CardFooter class="tw:flex-col tw:items-start tw:gap-4 tw:bg-muted!">
              <div class="tw:flex tw:w-full tw:flex-col tw:gap-2">
                <strong>Public Sector Partners</strong>
                <div class="tw:flex tw:flex-wrap tw:gap-2">
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">MDOT</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">SEMCOG</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">City of Ann Arbor, MI</Badge>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="tw:flex tw:w-full tw:flex-col tw:gap-2">
                <strong>Other Partners</strong>
                <div class="tw:flex tw:flex-wrap tw:gap-2">
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">General Motors</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">Ford</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">Volkswagen</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">Shulman Technology</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">Ouster</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">Verizon</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">AWS</Badge>
                  <Badge color="neutral" size="xl" variant="subtle">NVIDIA</Badge>
                </div>
              </div>
            </CardFooter>
          </Card>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tw:flex tw:flex-col tw:gap-4">
<Heading size="md">Key Research Questions</Heading>
      <ul class="program-list">
        <li>
          <em>Conflicts.</em> Can an ISS reliably predict conflicts among vehicles
          and vulnerable road users?
        </li>
        <ul>
          <li>How far in advance can conflicts be predicted?</li>
          <li>
            Under what conditions can reliable conflict predictions be made?
          </li>
        </ul>
        <li>
          <em>Unsafe Conditions.</em> Can an ISS reliably identify conflicts and characterize
          unsafe conditions?
        </li>
        <ul>
          <li>
            What types of unsafe conditions can be reliably identified or
            anticipated?
          </li>
          <li>
            How quickly can unsafe conditions be reliably identified or
            anticipated?
          </li>
        </ul>
        <li>
          <em>Mitigating Strategies.</em> Can an ISS take meaningful mitigating actions,
          either in the form of warnings or changes to intersection control, to mitigate
          conflicts and/or unsafe conditions?
        </li>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Can changes to intersection control be implemented in real-time to
            mitigate unsafe conditions (e.g., eliminating permissive vehicle
            turning movements traversing busy crosswalks)? What changes to
            control are most effective?
          </li>
          <li>
            Can warnings be issued in real-time to mitigate conflicts? What
            types of warnings are most effective (e.g., visual, auditory,
            haptic)?
          </li>
          <li>
            Can modifications to intersection control be utilized to mitigate
            individual conflicts (e.g., delay the illumination of a WALK signal
            until all approaching vehicles have safely cleared the crosswalk)?
          </li>
          <li>
            What are potential safety and congestion-reduction impacts resulting
            from these mitigating strategies?
          </li>
        </ul>
      </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="tw:flex tw:flex-col tw:gap-4">
        <Heading size="md">Additional Resources</Heading>
      <div
        class="tw:grid tw:grid-cols-1 tw:gap-6 tw:md:grid-cols-2 tw:lg:grid-cols-3"
      >
        <ResourceCard
          links={[
            {
              link: {
                href: "https://sam.gov/opp/8573bb118a5e4343a9512fed8b393d63/view",
                isExternal: true,
              },
              name: "Sources Sought Notice",
            },
          ]}
          subtitle="Issued on April 25, 2025 | Closed on May 9, 2025"
          title="Intersection Safety System (ISS) Prototyping"
        />
      </div>
      </div>
    </PageBody>
    </Page>
</Layout>
